    <!-- SECTION 1 -->
    <h2>The Real Reason You're Still Sore on Day Three</h2>

    <p>Here's what most men are told: "You're sore because you're getting older. Recovery just takes longer after 40. That's normal." And they accept it — because it feels true, and because nobody has offered them a better explanation.</p>

    <p>The research says otherwise. A 2025 meta-analysis in the <em>Journal of Aging and Physical Activity</em> pooled the studies comparing older and younger adults after the same muscle-damaging exercise. Older adults didn't lose more strength, didn't show more muscle damage, and actually reported <strong>less</strong> soreness than younger people. Age, by itself, isn't what's keeping you sore.</p>

    <p>So what is? For most men over 40, it's one of three things:</p>

    <ol>
      <li><strong>Irregular training.</strong> You lift for three weeks, miss two, then come back. Each return hits your muscles like a brand-new workout.</li>
      <li><strong>Big jumps in difficulty.</strong> New exercises, a lot more weight or volume, or a weekend of yard work your body isn't used to.</li>
      <li><strong>Heavy eccentric work.</strong> The lowering phase of a lift, walking downhill, or going down stairs causes the most soreness by far.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Protein comes in at the next step: once the soreness is over, it decides how well your muscles rebuild. If you're eating too little, you're doing the work and getting less out of it.</p>

    <!-- SECTION 2 — THE BIOLOGY -->
    <h2>The Biology of DOMS — What's Actually Happening</h2>

    <p>Delayed onset muscle soreness — DOMS — usually shows up 12 to 24 hours after a workout and peaks between 24 and 72 hours. That's why you often feel fine leaving the gym and wake up stiff the next morning. It's not lactic acid; that clears within an hour of training.</p>

    <div class="science-strip">
      <h3>What Happens After a Hard Workout — Step by Step</h3>
      <div class="science-item">
        <div class="science-dot"></div>
        <div class="science-text"><strong>Step 1 — Minor muscle damage.</strong> Eccentric movements (the lowering phase of a lift, running downhill, going down stairs) strain muscle fibers and the tissue around them the most. This is normal, and it's part of what tells the muscle to adapt.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="science-item">
        <div class="science-dot"></div>
        <div class="science-text"><strong>Step 2 — Inflammation and sensitized nerves.</strong> Over the next day, an inflammatory response brings repair cells into the area, and the muscle releases growth factors that make its nerve endings more sensitive. That sensitivity is a big part of why the muscle hurts when you move or press on it. It's also why how sore you feel doesn't line up neatly with how much damage was done.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="science-item">
        <div class="science-dot"></div>
        <div class="science-text"><strong>Step 3 — Repair and rebuilding.</strong> Your body uses amino acids from the protein you eat to repair and rebuild muscle proteins. Leucine is the amino acid that switches muscle protein synthesis on. Training keeps your muscles more responsive to protein for 24 hours or more.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="science-item">
        <div class="science-dot"></div>
        <div class="science-text"><strong>Step 4 — The muscle adapts.</strong> As soreness fades over a few days, the muscle and its connective tissue adapt so the same workout does less damage next time. That's the repeated bout effect, and it's the single most powerful protection against soreness.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

