    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">Why am I sore for 3 days after a workout after 40?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">Usually because the workout was new, harder than usual, or heavy on the lowering phase of each rep, not because of your age. Soreness normally peaks 24 to 72 hours after training and fades within a few days. A 2025 meta-analysis found older adults don't get more muscle damage or soreness than younger adults. The most common cause of 3-day soreness after 40 is irregular training: every time you come back after a break, your muscles react as if it's the first time.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">Does protein help with muscle soreness after 40?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">Protein helps recovery, but it doesn't do much for soreness itself. A 2023 meta-analysis found protein supplements did not reduce muscle soreness, though they did help men hold onto strength in the days after a hard workout. Protein's real job is rebuilding and strengthening the muscle so the next workout is easier on you. Training consistently is what reduces soreness the most.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">What is delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS)?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">Delayed onset muscle soreness is the stiffness and tenderness that shows up 12 to 24 hours after exercise and peaks around 24 to 72 hours. It comes from minor muscle damage, the inflammation that follows, and nerve endings in the muscle becoming more sensitive. It's strongest after new exercises and after the lowering (eccentric) part of a lift. It's normal, it isn't a sign of a good or bad workout, and it fades on its own.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">How much protein do I need to recover well from workouts after 40?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">Aim for at least 1.2 grams per kilogram of bodyweight per day, and about 1.6 g/kg if you lift regularly or are losing weight. For a 180-pound man (82 kg), that's about 98 to 131 grams a day. Spread it across 3 to 4 meals of roughly 30 to 40 grams each. Your daily total matters more than exact timing: muscles stay primed for 24 hours or more after training, so a protein meal within a few hours of your workout is plenty.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">Is it normal to be sore after every workout after 50?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">Some soreness after a new or harder workout is normal at any age. Being sore after every routine workout usually means your training is irregular or jumps in difficulty too quickly. Train the same movements 2 to 3 times a week and increase the load gradually, and soreness typically drops a lot within a few weeks. If you have severe pain, major swelling, or dark-colored urine after exercise, see a doctor, as those can be signs of a rare but serious condition called rhabdomyolysis.</div>
      </div>
